<article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1183/23120541.00763-2024</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">00763-2024</article-id><article-version vocab="JAV" vocab-identifier="http://www.niso.org/publications/rp/RP-8-2008.pdf" article-version-type="VoR">Version of Record</article-version><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Original Research Article</subject><subj-group><subject>Interstitial Lung Disease</subject></subj-group></subj-group><subj-group subj-group-type="hwp-journal-coll"><subject>12</subject><subject>13</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title><sup>1</sup>H-NMR urine metabolomic fingerprint for severity discrimination in pulmonary sarcoidosis</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid" authenticated="false">https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4070-5569</contrib-id><name><surname>Jeny</surname><given-names>Florence</given-names></name><xref rid="af1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="af2" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Triba</surname><given-names>Mohamed Nawfal</given-names></name><xref rid="af3" ref-type="aff">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Chauveau</surname><given-names>Simon</given-names></name><xref rid="af1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="af2" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Valeyre</surname><given-names>Dominique</given-names></name><xref rid="af1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="af2" ref-type="aff">2</xref><xref rid="af4" ref-type="aff">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mokhtari</surname><given-names>Sabrina</given-names></name><xref rid="af3" ref-type="aff">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nunes</surname><given-names>Hilario</given-names></name><xref rid="af1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="af2" ref-type="aff">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bernaudin</surname><given-names>Jean-Francois</given-names></name><xref rid="af1" ref-type="aff">1</xref><xref rid="af5" ref-type="aff">5</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><contrib-id contrib-id-type="orcid" authenticated="false">https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1451-2376</contrib-id><name><surname>Savarin</surname><given-names>Philippe</given-names></name><xref rid="af3" ref-type="aff">3</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp"/></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="af1"><label>1</label>Hypoxia and Lung Laboratory, UMR INSERM 1272, Sorbonne Paris Nord University, SMBH Bobigny, Bobigny, France</aff><aff id="af2"><label>2</label>APHP, Department of Pneumology, Avicenne Hospital, Bobigny, France</aff><aff id="af3"><label>3</label>Sorbonne Paris Nord University CB3S, UMR 7244, CNRS, Bobigny, France</aff><aff id="af4"><label>4</label>Paris Saint-Joseph Hospitals, Pulmonology Department, Paris, France</aff><aff id="af5"><label>5</label>Faculty of Medicine, Sorbonne University, Paris, France</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">Corresponding author: Philippe Savarin (<email xlink:href="philippe.savarin@univ-paris13.fr">philippe.savarin@univ-paris13.fr</email>)
</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>7</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>26</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>11</volume><issue>4</issue><elocation-id>00763-2024</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>29</day><month>7</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>30</day><month>1</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9;The authors 2025</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbynclicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This version is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution Non-Commercial Licence 4.0. For commercial reproduction rights and permissions contact <email xlink:href="mailto:permissions@ersnet.org">permissions@ersnet.org</email></license-p></license></permissions><abstract><sec><title>Background</title><p>Sarcoidosis is a systemic inflammatory immune disease characterised by noncaseating granulomas. Its course can vary from benign to very severe, requiring appropriate treatment. Few biomarkers are available to monitor the management of these patients and to identify those at risk of poor prognosis. Given the systemic nature of sarcoidosis, we hypothesised that the analysis of urine metabolites could provide valuable biomarkers for the management of severe disease.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods</title><p>We conducted a comparative analysis of urine metabolomics in a consecutive cohort of 37 well-phenotyped patients, using <sup>1</sup>H nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) with multivariate statistical analysis, followed by metabolite identification. After NMR spectra acquisition, we used principal component analysis and partial least squares (PLS) discriminant analysis to generate predictive models.</p></sec><sec><title>Results</title><p>A urinary metabolomic signature predictive of severe pulmonary sarcoidosis was identified using a PLS model. We selected five metabolites with significant changes in samples from severe sarcoidosis defined by a composite physiologic index &#x0003e;40 compared to those from nonsevere sarcoidosis. These changes correspond to the decreased levels of taurine, hippurate, serine and creatinine and increased levels of 3-hydroxyisovalerate. This metabolite profile suggests an association with activated inflammatory pathways.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusions</title><p>This study shows that urinary NMR metabolites can discriminate samples between nonsevere and severe sarcoidosis. It suggests that urinary metabolomic studies in sarcoidosis may be particularly useful to identify potentially relevant biomarkers. However, further validation in a larger cohort of patients at different disease stages is warranted to confirm the relevance of these NMR biomarkers for follow-up.</p></sec></abstract><abstract abstract-type="short" id="abstract-1"><title id="title-1">Shareable abstract</title><p><bold>Urinary metabolites analysed by <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy show a predictive signature associated with severe pulmonary sarcoidosis, suggesting an association with inflammation. It is important to test this as a potential prognostic biomarker in sarcoidosis.</bold>
<ext-link xlink:href="https://bit.ly/432YcgP" ext-link-type="uri">https://bit.ly/432YcgP</ext-link></p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><sec sec-type="intro" id="s1"><title>Introduction</title><p>Sarcoidosis is recognised as a systemic inflammatory immune disorder of multiple presumed aetiologies, characterised by the presence of characteristic noncaseating granulomas composed of epithelioid cells in tissue observations [<xref rid="C1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>]. It most commonly affects the respiratory and lymphatic systems, but virtually all organs may be involved, reflecting the extrapulmonary extent of the disease [<xref rid="C1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="C2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>]. Sarcoidosis can have different clinical consequences, ranging from benign to very severe and particularly aggressive sarcoidosis requiring sarcoidosis-specific treatment [<xref rid="C3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. Some patients do not respond to treatment or relapse, progressing to severe sarcoidosis with marked lung function impairment, such as pulmonary fibrosis and pulmonary hypertension. Demographic studies have suggested an increased risk of mortality associated with sarcoidosis, but this increased mortality rate is predominantly seen in subgroups of sarcoidosis patients classified as &#x0201c;severe or at risk&#x0201d;. Despite poor interobserver agreement, the Scadding radiographic classification [<xref rid="C4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>, <xref rid="C5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>] remains useful for predicting disease progression, while other findings are associated with an increased risk of mortality, such as pulmonary hypertension, pulmonary fibrosis and markedly altered lung function [<xref rid="C5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. Recently, a reliable prognostic algorithm based on the composite physiologic index (CPI), taking into account the ratio of main pulmonary artery diameter/ascending aorta diameter and the extent of fibrosis on chest computed tomography (CT), has been shown to accurately predict mortality [<xref rid="C5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. While various laboratory test abnormalities have been documented during the course of sarcoidosis, there is no consensus on which tests are best suited to assess disease activity, monitor treatment or evaluate prognosis [<xref rid="C8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>].</p><p>Metabolomics has been applied to a wide range of diseases, offering the potential to improve diagnostic accuracy and potentially stratify prognosis. A recent review has highlighted the contribution of metabolomics in sarcoidosis, highlighting the links with the gut or respiratory microbiome [<xref rid="C9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. This method has been used to study various physiological fluids under pathological conditions. In a previous study using <sup>1</sup>H nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, we investigated the metabolomics of saliva in patients with sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>].</p><p>Urine metabolomics has already been shown to be highly informative in several studies, especially in inflammatory diseases and cancer [<xref rid="C11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>, <xref rid="C12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>]. For example, urine autotaxin levels may reflect disease activity in sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>]. While several <sup>1</sup>H-NMR studies of sarcoidosis patients have been reported in saliva or serum [<xref rid="C10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>, <xref rid="C14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>], to the best of our knowledge, urine has not yet been investigated in sarcoidosis.</p><p>Therefore, in this pilot study, we conducted a <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy analysis in a consecutive cohort of well-phenotyped sarcoidosis patients to identify specific changes in urinary metabolite content associated with the severity in pulmonary sarcoidosis, as previously demonstrated for other systemic diseases [<xref rid="C19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="C21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>].</p><p>We aimed to identify discriminative urinary metabolites in a sarcoidosis population to assess their accuracy in identifying poor prognosis in relation to the CPI score. Therefore, urine metabolites were compared according to the status of severe pulmonary sarcoidosis. We found that <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy analysis of urine samples could identify a significant metabolomic signature associated with the severity of pulmonary sarcoidosis most likely related to inflammatory pathways. These results need to be confirmed by larger and multicentre cohort studies.</p></sec><sec id="s2"><title>Study population and methods</title><sec id="s2a"><title>Study population</title><p>Urine samples were prospectively collected from consecutive chronic and recently diagnosed sarcoidosis patients between January 2018 and October 2019. All study patients were cared for at the Respiratory Medicine Department of the APHP Avicenne University Hospital (Bobigny, France), which is an expert centre for sarcoidosis. All included patients fulfilled the American Thoracic Society/European Respiratory Society/World Association of Sarcoidosis and Other Granulomatous Disorders diagnostic criteria for sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="C22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>]. None of them had a history of systemic corticosteroid, hydroxychloroquine sulphate or immunosuppressive therapy in the previous 6&#x02005;months. Other study exclusion criteria included age under 18&#x02005;years, diabetes, use of a standard treatment regimen (including recent treatments such as antibiotics), urinary tract infection or urinary lithiasis at the time of sampling, and renal insufficiency. In accordance with French legislation, written informed consent was required from patients prior to enrolment. Approval was obtained from the French National Committee for the Protection of Human Subjects (2017/33;2017-A01555-48).</p><p>Data collected at the time of urine collection are shown in <xref rid="TB1" ref-type="table">table 1</xref>. Respiratory function tests, forced vital capacity (FVC), forced expiratory volume in 1&#x02005;s (FEV<sub>1</sub>) and diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide (<italic toggle="yes">D</italic><sub>LCO</sub>) were expressed as % of predicted values. The CPI was calculated according to the following formula: CPI=91&#x02212;(0.65&#x000d7;<italic toggle="yes">D</italic><sub>LCO</sub>)&#x02212;(0.53&#x000d7;FVC)+(0.34&#x000d7;FEV<sub>1</sub>) for each patient [<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. As previously reported, a CPI greater than 40 is considered to identify patients with poor vital prognosis, representing the so-called &#x0201c;severe sarcoidosis&#x0201d; phenotype.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="TB1"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Clinical data concerning the investigated patients with pulmonary sarcoidosis</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Population</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">n=37</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
