</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">75.5 (53.2&#x02013;121.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>ACE &#x0003e;ULN</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">21 (56.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Blood lymphocytes (per mm<sup>3</sup>)</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1093 (775&#x02013;1530)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>FVC (% pred)</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">89.4&#x000b1;19.0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>FEV<sub>1</sub> (% pred)</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">83.2&#x000b1;18.2</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold><italic toggle="yes">D</italic><sub>LCO</sub> (% pred)</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">71.4&#x000b1;16.9</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>CPI</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">23.0 (16.9&#x02013;34.0)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>CPI &#x0003e;40</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (16.2)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Treatment initiation (after urine sampling)</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">23 (62.2)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Results are expressed as n (%), median (interquartile range) or mean&#x000b1;<sc>sd</sc>. Forced vital capacity (FVC), forced expiratory volume in 1&#x02005;s (FEV<sub>1</sub>) and diffusing capacity of the lung for carbon monoxide (<italic toggle="yes">D</italic><sub>LCO</sub>) are expressed as percentages of predicted values. ACE: angiotensin converting enzyme; BMI: body mass index; CPI: composite physiologic index; FAS: fatigue assessment scale; ULN: upper limit of normal. <bold><sup>#</sup></bold>: FAS score was calculated for 36 patients according to [<xref rid="C44" ref-type="bibr">44</xref>].</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>Prior to urine collection, in order to limit the various factors that could influence metabolite analysis, patients were advised to reduce their fluid intake (<italic toggle="yes">e.g.</italic> water) and to avoid meat, coffee, tea, alcohol consumption and exercise the day before. They were also asked to avoid grilled meat and smoked fish for 48&#x02005;h. Daily diet and habits that could interfere with the metabolomics results were collected (<xref rid="TB2" ref-type="table">table 2</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="TB2"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Data on main daily habits</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Population</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">n=37</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Daily treatment</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (10.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Analgesic treatment</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 (54.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Antibiotics in the last month</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (5.4)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Hormone replacement therapy</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (5.4)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Meat consumption &#x0003e;once a day</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (24.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Grilled meat consumption</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (10.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Alcohol &#x0003e;1 glass per day</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 (3.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Tea or herbal tea &#x0003e;3 cups per day</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (10.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Coffee &#x0003e;3 cups per day</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (24.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Spicy food &#x0003e;once a day</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9 (24.3)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Results are displayed as n (%).</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec id="s2b"><title><sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy method and multivariate analyses</title><sec id="s2b1"><title>Urine collection</title><p>The first morning urine sample was collected (100&#x02013;200&#x02005;mL) into sterile urine containers and immediately centrifuged at 4&#x02005;&#x000b0;C at the hospital. Filters (0.22&#x02005;&#x000b5;m) were rinsed with distilled water and used for the supernatant. pH was measured and adjusted to 6.8 and 5 mL samples were stored at &#x02212;80&#x02005;&#x000b0;C prior to NMR analysis. At the time of analysis, 100&#x02005;&#x003bc;L of deuterium oxide (Eurisotop) was added to 600&#x02005;&#x003bc;L of thawed urine.</p></sec><sec id="s2b2"><title>Sample preparation and NMR analysis</title><p>NMR profiling was performed using a 500&#x02005;MHz Advance III Bruker NMR spectrometer (BioSpin Bruker, Wissembourg, France) on an automatic 24-position sample changer with cooling capability using a quadruple resonance inverse probe. The NMR spectra were acquired using the noesy1dgppr sequence at 298 K. The spectral width was set at 10 kHz. A Fourier transform was performed on the NMR data with the line broadening set at 0.3&#x02005;Hz. All spectra were phased and baseline-corrected, followed by alignment. Finally, each one-dimensional (1D) NMR spectrum was sliced into 0.001&#x02005;ppm buckets, containing NMR signals. These steps were performed using NMRPipe software. Prior to statistical analysis, the NMR spectra bins were normalised using the probabilistic quotient normalisation algorithm [<xref rid="C23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>] and unit variance scaled. The assignment of the <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectra was performed using two-dimensional experiments, Chenomx software and the Human Metabolome Database (version 5.0).</p><p>A principal component analysis (PCA) model was generated using an in-house MATLAB&#x000ae; script. The aim was to exclude possible outliers. A partial least squares (PLS) discriminant model was used to identify fingerprints related to patients&#x02019; age and sarcoidosis severity using CPI values as the Y-variable. Statistical analysis and visualisation were performed on MATLAB&#x000ae; R2016b for macOS (Mathworks, Natick, MA, USA) with scripts based on the method described by T<sc>rygg</sc> and W<sc>old</sc> [<xref rid="C24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>]. Models were evaluated using R<sup>2</sup> (Y). Q<sup>2</sup> (Y) was calculated using seven-fold cross-validation. Q<sup>2</sup> (Y) estimates the predictability of the model. The score plot, showing the discrimination between groups, and the loading plot, showing the spectral region responsible for this discrimination, have been calculated.</p></sec></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="s3"><title>Results</title><sec id="s3a"><title>Patients</title><p>42 consecutive patients were initially enrolled in the study. After acquisition of the <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy data, it was determined that five urine samples had poor water suppression and a low signal-to-noise ratio that did not allow analysis. These samples were excluded from the final study, which was therefore performed on the samples of 37 patients. <xref rid="TB1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref> shows the characteristics of the patients. Most were men (70%) and 19% were current smokers.</p><p>Patients with a &#x0201c;recent diagnosis&#x0201d; of sarcoidosis (<italic toggle="yes">i.e.</italic> within 2&#x02005;years following the urine sample) represented 62.2% of the population (n=23). Patients with a chronic sarcoidosis (<italic toggle="yes">i.e.</italic> diagnosed 3&#x02005;years or more before the urine sample) represented 37.8% (n=14).</p><p>Patients had an extrapulmonary involvement in 54%. The mean CPI was 23.9&#x000b1;15.0 and six patients (16%) had a CPI above 40, indicating severe sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. It should be emphasised that 62% of the patients required a steroid and/or immunosuppressive treatment initiation (started after urine collection). The CPI of patients with a &#x0201c;recent diagnosis&#x0201d; of sarcoidosis (25.2&#x000b1;14.1) was not statistically different from that of patients with &#x0201c;chronic&#x0201d; sarcoidosis (26.0&#x000b1;13.8). Similarly, the number of patients receiving treatment after urine collection did not differ between the two groups. All 37 patients followed the recommendations described above for the days leading up to urine collection. <xref rid="TB2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref> presents data on the most important daily habits. The interference of each of these parameters with the urine metabolite results was tested.</p></sec><sec id="s3b"><title><sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy results</title><p><sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectra were recorded at 298 K for urine samples. The variability observed between the different samples was first investigated using unsupervised statistical analysis. We tested variability related to sex and age, as previously reported in urine samples from a cohort of healthy men and women [<xref rid="C25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]. The PCA results of our samples show that a part of the variability could be related to sex (<xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">figure 1a</xref>)<italic toggle="yes">.</italic> Subsequently, supervised analysis was performed to investigate the potential variability according to age, as previously investigated in other pathologies [<xref rid="C26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref>]. A PLS model with three components was constructed (the score and the loading plots are shown in <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">figure 1b</xref>). With the 37 urine spectra, we obtained a Q<sup>2</sup> of 0.55, indicating the model's ability to predict the age of the patients.</p><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>a) Principal component analysis score plot and corresponding loading plot of the 37 <sup>1</sup>H nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) urine spectra from the urine of patients with severe pulmonary sarcoidosis, illustrating the differences in gender fingerprints (females: yellow dots; males: blue dots). b) Partial least squares (PLS) score plot and corresponding loading plot of the 37 <sup>1</sup>H-NMR urine spectra. The first predictive <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis discriminates urine profiles according to patient age. Three components were calculated, Q<sup>2</sup>=0.55. T1: scores of the first principal component (PC1); T2: scores of the second principal component (PC2); Ppca1: loadings of PC1 (principal component analysis); Wpls1: weights of the first PLS component.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="00763-2024.01" position="float"/></fig><p>As a third step, we searched for biomarkers that correlate with the severity of pulmonary sarcoidosis. It has been shown that the CPI could be considered a significant parameter for predicting mortality in fibrosing sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. For that purpose, the data were randomly divided into two sets of results. First, a set of 29 spectra was used as a cross-validation training set and a second set of eight urine spectra was used as a test set. A PLS model with four components was identified using the CPI as the Y-variable. The R<sup>2</sup>X was 0.275, the R<sup>2</sup>Y was 0.997 and the Q<sup>2</sup> was 0.68. We tested this model on the test set and an R<sup>2</sup> of prediction was calculated. For each cross-validation submodel, the prediction R<sup>2</sup> of the test set was calculated. These submodel R<sup>2</sup> results were used to build the confidence interval for the global prediction R<sup>2</sup> of the test set. We obtained a value of 0.74 (95% CI 0.62&#x02013;0.84) for this model, showing the significant quality of the prediction. The resulting score plot is shown in <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">figure 2a</xref> and the corresponding loading plot is shown in <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">figure 2b</xref>. A permutation test (500 permutations) was performed to evaluate the robustness of the model (<xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">figure 2c</xref>). <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2d</xref> presents the predicted CPI (circle) <italic toggle="yes">versus</italic> the real CPI (cross). Confidence intervals in the CPI prediction for the test set were obtained using the cross-validation submodels. A prediction of the CPI for the test set was calculated by each submodel, allowing the estimation of a confidence interval. <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">Figure 2e</xref> shows the projection of the sample showing the predictive power of the model. The first five discriminant metabolites with the highest variable importance in projection score in the PLS model (<xref rid="TB3" ref-type="table">table 3</xref>) were selected to build a logistic model. These metabolites were used as biomarkers to predict whether the CPI was higher than 40, since this value of the CPI is considered critical [<xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. Boxplots of corresponding intensities are presented in <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">figure 3a</xref>. After unit variance scaling of the five selected signals, a logistic regression was performed (the coefficients used in the logistic regression are shown in <xref rid="TB3" ref-type="table">table 3</xref>). In patients with a CPI higher than 40, a decrease in the concentration of taurine, hippurate, serine and creatinine and an increase in 3-hydroxyisovalerate is observed. A receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.89 (0.62&#x02013;1.0) (<xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">figure 3b</xref>) was obtained on the training set. The confidence interval was obtained by bootstrap resampling. All eight urine samples in the test set were correctly predicted, demonstrating the strong performance and potential utility of this biomarker. The relationship between age and CPI score was tested by calculating the p-value of the Spearman rank correlation coefficient (0.19). This value suggests that there is no significant relationship between these two variables. The difference in CPI scores between men and women was assessed using the Mann&#x02013;Whitney U test. The p-value (0.28) suggests that there is no significant difference between these two distributions.</p><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>a) Partial least squares (PLS) score plot and b) corresponding loading plot of the 29 <sup>1</sup>H nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra of the training set. The first predictive <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis discriminates the urine profiles according to the composite physiologic index (CPI) score. Four components were calculated (R<sup>2</sup>X=0.28, Q<sup>2</sup>=0.67). Wpls1: weights of the first PLS component. c) Values of R<sup>2</sup> and Q<sup>2</sup> after 500 permutation tests. d) Application of the calculated model to the eight one-dimensional (1D) NMR urine spectra and projection. The <italic toggle="yes">y</italic>-axis corresponds to the prediction axis (CPI) and the <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>-axis corresponds to the sample number. The projection of the 1D spectrum corresponds to the circle and is compared to the CPI value (cross). e) Projection of the eight 1D NMR urine spectra of the test set onto the model.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="00763-2024.02" position="float"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="TB3"><label>TABLE 3</label><caption><p>The five metabolites with the highest variable importance in projection (VIP) scores, showing their chemical shift, Spearman correlation and logistic regression results</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="2" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="2" colspan="1">Chemical shift, ppm</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" colspan="1">VIP score</th><th align="center" colspan="2" rowspan="1">Spearman correlation</th><th align="center" colspan="2" rowspan="1">Logistic regression</th></tr><tr><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Coefficient</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-value</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Coefficient</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">p-value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Taurine</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.29</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.14</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.34</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.03</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;3.95</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0387</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Hippurate</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.87</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.14</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.55</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0004</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;1.48</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0179</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>3-Hydroxyisovalerate</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.29</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.69</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.42</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.22</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.2467</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Serine</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.98</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.58</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.40</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.01</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.64</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.2012</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Creatinine</bold>
</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.06</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.48</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.10</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.52</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;1.51</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1615</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>a) Boxplots of the signal intensities of the selected metabolites, from the left to right: 3-hydroxyisovalerate, creatinine, taurine, serine and hippurate. b) Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve obtained on the training test set using the logistic regression. ROC curve area under the curve: 0.89 (95% CI 0.62&#x02013;1.0).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="00763-2024.03" position="float"/></fig></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="s4"><title>Discussion</title><p>Metabolomics studies in sarcoidosis are rather scarce. A review by K<sc>im</sc>
<italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. [<xref rid="C9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>] very clearly presented the potential benefits of metabolomics to decipher the links between sarcoidosis and the metabolome of the microbiome, whether oral, intestinal or pulmonary. For diagnosis purposes, serum metabolic disturbances have demonstrated very high sensitivity and specificity in differentiating sarcoidosis from tuberculosis [<xref rid="C16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>]. Therefore, we designed the present study to identify preliminary urinary metabolites that could be considered as severity indexes and prognostic biomarkers. We chose to investigate urinary metabolites using NMR spectroscopy, which has not been yet reported in sarcoidosis.</p><p>NMR spectroscopy is one of the main analytical techniques for metabolomics, with the advantages of minimal sample preparation and high reproducibility [<xref rid="C27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref>]. In addition, urine sampling is very easy, noninvasive and can be repeated. We chose to collect the first morning void urine at the time of the hospital visit, following all recommendations required for standard practice [<xref rid="C28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. We have maximally controlled the experimental conditions to exclude bacterial contamination by 0.22&#x02005;&#x000b5;m filtration and to ensure metabolite stability by storage at &#x02212;80&#x02005;&#x000b0;C prior to analysis, with no significant delay between urination and sample processing [<xref rid="C28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>]. Patients were also asked to refrain from meat, coffee, tea and alcohol consumption the day before, as well as sporting activities. Patients were also asked to avoid eating grilled meat and smoked fish for 48&#x02005;h before the test. Daily diet and habits that could interfere with the metabolomic results were recorded and checked to ensure that they did not interfere with the metabolites identified in this study.</p><p>The CPI was originally developed to assess idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, another fibrotic interstitial lung disease [<xref rid="C29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>]. Regression analysis was used to generate the weighted combination of lung function variables that best matched the extent of pulmonary fibrosis on CT [<xref rid="C29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>]. In sarcoidosis, a CPI greater than 40 was found to be a robust predictor of mortality in two different cohorts, with hazard ratios ranging from 3.8 to 4.2 [<xref rid="C6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>, <xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. It should be noted that no patient in our cohort had pulmonary hypertension or fibrosis greater than 20% on high-resolution CT, which are two other prognostic factors associated with reduced survival. This reinforces the accuracy of CPI selection in this study [<xref rid="C6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>, <xref rid="C7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>]. In our patients, a high CPI is more likely to be associated with extended granulomatous lung inflammation than with the fibrotic process. This is consistent with the observed set of discriminant metabolites reflecting inflammation.</p><p>Five discriminant urinary metabolites, <italic toggle="yes">i.e.</italic> a decrease in taurine, hippurate, serine and creatinine and an increase of 3-hydroxyisovalerate, were identified by PLS and selected for their association with a poor prognostic CPI score, <italic toggle="yes">i.e.</italic> a CPI above 40 compared to patients with a CPI below 40.</p><p>Taurine is a semi-essential amino acid that plays a role in the regulation of oxidative stress and proinflammatory cytokines [<xref rid="C30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>]. In serum, taurine levels were not significantly different between sarcoidosis patients and healthy volunteers [<xref rid="C15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], and an increase was even observed in veterans with sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>]. However, in these studies, sarcoidosis patients were considered globally and disease severity was not a specific objective. The decrease in taurine we observed in urine collected from patients with severe sarcoidosis is consistent with reports from studies in patients with inflammatory bowel disease [<xref rid="C21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>].</p><p>Hippurate is one of the most abundant microbial host cometabolites [<xref rid="C31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>]. A large study of urinary metabolites in immune-mediated inflammatory diseases found a highly significant association between a decreased hippurate level and two inflammatory bowel diseases, <italic toggle="yes">i.e</italic>. ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease [<xref rid="C19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="C21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>]. Furthermore, in a study of patients with severe fever with thrombocytopaenia syndrome, a decrease in urinary hippuric acid was observed, while an increase was observed in cases with fatal progression [<xref rid="C32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref>]. In addition, low urinary hippurate has been reported in patients with acute pancreatitis [<xref rid="C33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref>] or in obesity, which has been suggested to be associated with the gut microbiome dysbiosis [<xref rid="C34" ref-type="bibr">34</xref>].</p><p>Serine, a nonessential amino acid, is implicated in numerous different metabolic processes, such as antioxidant defence and regulation of metabolic homeostasis [<xref rid="C35" ref-type="bibr">35</xref>]. Serine alterations have been reported in inflammatory bowel disease, both in patients and in experimental models in mice [<xref rid="C36" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>]. A low level of urinary serine has been observed by mass spectrometry in children with Crohn disease compared to those with ulcerative colitis [<xref rid="C37" ref-type="bibr">37</xref>] and recently, in a mouse model of colitis, inhibition of serine synthesis or depletion played a role in modulating acute or chronic colonic inflammation [<xref rid="C36" ref-type="bibr">36</xref>]. Recent studies have shown that serine metabolism is related to macrophage functions; for example, exogenous serine supports M1 macrophage polarisation through mammalian target of rapamycin signalling [<xref rid="C38" ref-type="bibr">38</xref>], which is a key pathway in sarcoidosis [<xref rid="C39" ref-type="bibr">39</xref>]. However, in contrast with the study by M<sc>irsaeidi</sc>
<italic toggle="yes">et al.</italic> [<xref rid="C17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>], an increase in serum N-stearoyl serine was found in patients with a fibrosing lung pattern.</p><p>In the present study, low urinary creatinine excretion was found in patients with severe sarcoidosis. At steady state, urinary creatinine excretion is one of the most specific indices of total body muscle mass [<xref rid="C40" ref-type="bibr">40</xref>]. These results should be considered in of the light of a previous study of 423 Dutch patients that showed that muscle wasting was present in 25% of sarcoidosis patients and was associated with more severe pulmonary disease [<xref rid="C41" ref-type="bibr">41</xref>].</p><p>In contrast, an increase in urinary 3-hydroxyisovalerate was found in patients with a CPI greater than 40, <italic toggle="yes">i.e.</italic> severe sarcoidosis. 3-Hydroxyisovaleric acid, is a byproduct of the leucine catabolic pathway, which has also been implicated in muscle protein catabolism in response to energy expenditure and inflammation [<xref rid="C42" ref-type="bibr">42</xref>]. Such an increase of 3-hydroxyisovalerate has been found in the urine of patients with Crohn disease [<xref rid="C19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]. In addition, 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid has been found to increase under hypoxic conditions [<xref rid="C43" ref-type="bibr">43</xref>]. Consistent with our findings, increased serum levels of 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid have been described in patients with severe COVID-19 and impaired lung function [<xref rid="C43" ref-type="bibr">43</xref>]. This increase has been suggested to be secondary to an altered mitochondrial branched-chain amino acid metabolism related to hypoxia [<xref rid="C43" ref-type="bibr">43</xref>] and activation of the gluconeogenic pathway. Indeed, COVID-19 severity and increased muscle catabolism have been associated with increased gluconeogenesis [<xref rid="C43" ref-type="bibr">43</xref>], which further supports our findings.</p><p>Considering the aggregate of these five main discriminating metabolites, it can be hypothesised that they are associated with the inflammatory status observed in severe sarcoidosis and point to a link between the gut microbiome and pulmonary sarcoidosis, the gut&#x02013;lung axis highlighted by K<sc>im</sc>
<italic toggle="yes">et al</italic>. [<xref rid="C9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>].</p><p>In conclusion, the present pilot study using urinary metabolite identification by high-resolution <sup>1</sup>H-NMR spectroscopy coupled with multivariate statistical analysis identified a metabolic signature significant for severe pulmonary sarcoidosis consisting of five main discriminating metabolites (decrease of taurine, hippurate, serine, creatinine and increase of 3-hydroxyisovalerate). The five identified metabolites formed an aggregated variable that achieved the best positive predictive value for the diagnosis of severe sarcoidosis according to the CPI, an index to identify severe pulmonary sarcoidosis. The results of this pilot study demonstrating the potential of urinary metabolomics need to be confirmed in validation cohorts and through longitudinal sampling. Therefore, the simple and robust NMR spectroscopy of urine samples may be of significant assistance in the follow-up of sarcoidosis patients and in monitoring treatment decisions.</p></sec></body><back><ack><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>The authors would like to thank Prof. Val&#x000e9;rie Besnard, Carole Plan&#x000e8;s, Yurdagul Uzunhan, Dr Morgane Didier, Cecile Rotenberg, Aur&#x000e9;lie Herv&#x000e9;, Nadia Bouchemal, Tony Palama and the nurses of the Pneumology Department of the Avicenne Hospital for their active collaboration in the management of the study. They also thank Nacira Darghal of the Clinical Research Unit of Avicenne Hospital for the APHP's support of the study. They acknowledge the NMR-PF facility (Sorbonne Paris Nord University, France).</p></ack><fn-group><fn><p>This article has been revised according to the correction published in <italic toggle="yes">ERJ Open Res</italic> 2025; 11: 50763-2024 [<ext-link xlink:href="https://doi.org/10.1183/23120541.50763-2024" ext-link-type="uri">https://doi.org/10.1183/23120541.50763-2024</ext-link>].</p></fn></fn-group><fn-group><fn><p>Provenance: Submitted article, peer reviewed.</p></fn><fn fn-type="other"><p>This observational study is prospectively registered with <ext-link xlink:href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/" ext-link-type="uri">ClinicalTrials.gov</ext-link> as <related-object source-type="clinical-trials-registry" source-id="ClinicalTrials.gov" source-id-type="registry-name" document-id="NCT05181930" document-id-type="clinical-trial-number" xlink:href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05181930&#x0000d;&#x00026;" id="RO1">NCT05181930</related-object>.</p></fn><fn><p>Ethics statement: The Committee for the Protection of Persons of France South West has issued a favourable opinion (number 2017/33;2017-A01555-48).</p></fn><fn><p>Author contributions: All the authors contributed to study design, data acquisition, data interpretation and writing of the manuscript.</p></fn><fn fn-type="COI-statement"><p>Conflict of interest: The authors have nothing to disclose.</p></fn><fn fn-type="financial-disclosure"><p>Support statement: This study was supported by the Sorbonne Paris Nord University Research Fund (grant IFRB Sorbonne Paris Nord University 2017).</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="C1"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><string-name><surname>Crouser</surname>
